  "name": "Final Report to the Outer Continental Shelf Environmental Assessment Program (OCSEAP): Diets and Food Web Relationships of Seabirds in the Gulf of Alaska and Adjacent Marine Regions",
  "description": "This report references diet data of harlequin ducks (Histrionicus histrionicus), nearshore bird communities, and scoters (Melanitta spp.) and diet and population abundance data of fish species: Ammodytes hexapterus and Hexagrammos lagocephalus, invertebrate communities, invertebrate species: Clinocardium spp., Littorina sitkana, Macoma spp., Mya spp., Mytilus trossulus, Protothaca staminea, Saxidomus spp., Spisula polynyma, Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis, and Telmessus cheiragonus, and nearshore fish communities from the Gulf of Alaska. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Sanger, G.A. and R.D. Jones, Jr. 1981. Final report to the Outer Continental Shelf Environmental Assessment Program (OCSEAP): diets and food web relationships of seabirds in the Gulf of Alaska and adjacent marine regions. U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Research - Migratory Bird Section, Contract # 01-5-022-2538, Anchorage, Alaska. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #868 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23566239",
